{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>Only take heed to yourself, etc. lest you forget the things</b>. \"Then when you do not forget them and do them in their true form, you will be accounted wise and discerning, and if you distort them out of forgetfulness, you will be accounted fools\" — this is the language of Rashi. (Rashi on Deuteronomy 4:9) And it is not correct at all, but this verse, in my opinion, is a negative commandment, and it warns about it most emphatically. For when He said that one must be careful in all the commandments and observe the statutes and the ordinances to do them, He returned and said: only I warn you most emphatically to be careful and to guard yourself very, very much, to remember from whence the commandments came to you — that you not forget the standing at Mount Sinai from all the things that your eyes saw: the voices and the torches, His glory and His greatness, and His words that you heard there from amid the fire. And you shall make known all the things that your eyes saw at that glorious assembly to your children and your children's children forever. And He explained the reason, for HaShem established that assembly so that you would learn to fear Him all your days, and your children you shall teach for all generations — if so, do you thus, and do not forget it.\n\nNow, before He mentions the commandments that were said there, He warned in a negative commandment that we not forget any matter from that assembly and not remove it from our hearts forever, and He commanded in a positive commandment that we make it known to all our seed, from generation to generation, everything that was there in sight and in hearing.\n\nAnd the benefit of this commandment is very great. For if the words of the Torah came to us from the mouth of Moses alone, even though his prophecy was verified by signs and wonders, if a prophet or a dreamer of dreams arises among us and commands us in the opposite of the Torah, and gives us a sign or wonder, doubt would enter people's hearts. But when the Torah reaches us from the mouth of the Gevurah to our ears, and our eyes see — there is no intermediary — we will deny every dissenter and every doubter, and we will declare him a liar. No sign will avail him and no wonder will save him from death at our hands, for we know his falsehood. This is what He said there (Exodus 19:9): <b>and also in you they will believe forever</b>. For when we transmit the matter as well to our children, they will know that the matter was true without doubt, as though all the generations saw it — for we would not testify falsely to our children, nor would we bequeath to them a vain and useless thing. And they will not doubt at all the testimony that we testify to them, but they will believe with certainty that we all saw with our own eyes everything that we relate to them.\n\nAnd this matter will come up further in the pericope of (Deuteronomy 13:2): \"If there arises among you a prophet or a dreamer of dreams, etc.,\" and I have already mentioned it there in the pericope of <b>In the third month</b> (Exodus 19:9)."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {0 "הגבורה (the Gevurah) rendered as a divine epithet per the voice rule of not translating names of God into English words; it is used here by Ramban as a name for the divine."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:45:19.585393973Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}